SELECT 'eb782b6f-852b-73bc-841b-a53db9bcd9a4', '1e14527f-0f85-747d-b7d3-62e565c065d0', 'SQL Sublanguages: DDL, DML, DQL, DCL, TCL', 'SQL Sublanguages: DDL, DML, DQL, DCL, TCL', 'SQL commands are grouped by what they act on — the structure, the data, permissions, or transactions. A classic first interview question.
Group	Stands for	Commands	Acts on
DDL	Data Definition Language	CREATE, ALTER, DROP, TRUNCATE, RENAME	Schema objects (tables, indexes, views)
DML	Data Manipulation Language	INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE	Rows inside tables
DQL	Data Query Language	SELECT	Reading rows (often counted as part of DML)
DCL	Data Control Language	GRANT, REVOKE	Permissions of users and roles
TCL	Transaction Control Language	COMMIT, ROLLBACK, SAVEPOINT, BEGIN	Grouping DML into atomic units
One of each
-- DDL: define structure
CREATE TABLE notes (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, body TEXT NOT NULL);

-- TCL + DML: change data inside a transaction
BEGIN;
INSERT INTO notes (body) VALUES (''learn SQL''), (''practice joins'');
UPDATE notes SET body = ''practice window functions'' WHERE id = 2;
COMMIT;

-- DQL: read it back
SELECT id, body FROM notes ORDER BY id;
id | body
---+--------------------------
1  | learn SQL
2  | practice window functions
-- DCL: permissions (server databases only; SQLite has no users)
GRANT SELECT, INSERT ON notes TO analyst;
REVOKE INSERT ON notes FROM analyst;
Why the grouping matters
Auto-commit of DDL
In MySQL and Oracle every DDL statement implicitly commits the open transaction and cannot be rolled back. PostgreSQL and SQLite allow DDL inside a transaction.
Permissions
DBAs grant DDL rights to few people (schema owners), DML to applications, and read-only DQL to analysts.
Is TRUNCATE DDL or DML?
DDL in most engines. It removes all rows by deallocating data pages instead of deleting row by row, does not fire DELETE triggers, resets identity counters, and (in MySQL/Oracle) auto-commits. DELETE is DML: row by row, logged, can have a WHERE, can be rolled back.
Is SELECT part of DML?
Formally the SQL standard puts SELECT under data manipulation; many courses split it out as DQL. Either answer is accepted if you explain that SELECT only reads data.',
  setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'SQL Sublanguages: DDL, DML, DQL, DCL, TCL'), 'A')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'SQL Sublanguages: DDL, DML, DQL, DCL, TCL'), 'B')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'SQL commands are grouped by what they act on — the structure, the data, permissions, or transactions. A classic first interview question.
Group	Stands for	Commands	Acts on
DDL	Data Definition Language	CREATE, ALTER, DROP, TRUNCATE, RENAME	Schema objects (tables, indexes, views)
DML	Data Manipulation Language	INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE	Rows inside tables
DQL	Data Query Language	SELECT	Reading rows (often counted as part of DML)
DCL	Data Control Language	GRANT, REVOKE	Permissions of users and roles
TCL	Transaction Control Language	COMMIT, ROLLBACK, SAVEPOINT, BEGIN	Grouping DML into atomic units
One of each
-- DDL: define structure
CREATE TABLE notes (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, body TEXT NOT NULL);

-- TCL + DML: change data inside a transaction
BEGIN;
INSERT INTO notes (body) VALUES (''learn SQL''), (''practice joins'');
UPDATE notes SET body = ''practice window functions'' WHERE id = 2;
COMMIT;

-- DQL: read it back
SELECT id, body FROM notes ORDER BY id;
id | body
---+--------------------------
1  | learn SQL
2  | practice window functions
-- DCL: permissions (server databases only; SQLite has no users)
GRANT SELECT, INSERT ON notes TO analyst;
REVOKE INSERT ON notes FROM analyst;
Why the grouping matters
Auto-commit of DDL
In MySQL and Oracle every DDL statement implicitly commits the open transaction and cannot be rolled back. PostgreSQL and SQLite allow DDL inside a transaction.
Permissions
DBAs grant DDL rights to few people (schema owners), DML to applications, and read-only DQL to analysts.
Is TRUNCATE DDL or DML?
DDL in most engines. It removes all rows by deallocating data pages instead of deleting row by row, does not fire DELETE triggers, resets identity counters, and (in MySQL/Oracle) auto-commits. DELETE is DML: row by row, logged, can have a WHERE, can be rolled back.
Is SELECT part of DML?
Formally the SQL standard puts SELECT under data manipulation; many courses split it out as DQL. Either answer is accepted if you explain that SELECT only reads data.'), 'C'),
